  "estimatedMinutes": 1,
  "statement": "If \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are real numbers, the equation \\(3x-5+a=bx+1\\) has a unique solution \\(x\\) [the symbol \\(a\\ne0\\) means that \\(a\\) is different from zero]:",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "for all \\(a\\) and \\(b\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "if \\(a\\ne2b\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "if \\(a\\ne6\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "if \\(b\\ne0\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "if \\(b\\ne3\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "e",
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1960-p14.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Rearranging gives \\[ (3-b)x=6-a. \\] This has a unique solution exactly when \\(3-b\\ne0,\\) or \\(b\\ne3.\\)\n\nThus, the correct answer is E.",
      "hints": [
        "Collect the \\(x\\)-terms on one side",
        "A linear equation has a unique solution when the coefficient of \\(x\\) is nonzero"
      ],
